# Project Rules for Cline

## Scope of changes
- Only modify the files and functions directly relevant to the request. Do not touch unrelated code.
- Do not refactor, rename, or "clean up" code that wasn't part of the request, even if it looks improvable.
- Prefer the smallest diff that correctly solves the problem.
- If a change requires touching a file outside what was mentioned, stop and ask first.

## Comments & documentation
- Do not add comments unless explicitly asked, or the logic is genuinely non-obvious (complex regex, non-standard algorithm, workaround for a bug).
- Do not add docstrings/JSDoc to every function by default — only where the project already does this consistently.
- No "TODO" or placeholder comments unless requested.

## Code style
- Match the existing code style, naming conventions, and formatting already used in the file/project. Do not impose a different style.
- Do not introduce new dependencies/libraries without asking first, even if they'd simplify the task.
- Do not add error handling, logging, retries, or validation beyond what's asked, unless the file already follows that pattern elsewhere.
- Keep functions and files consistent with the existing architecture (e.g. if the project uses a services/ layer, don't put logic in controllers).

## Security & data handling
- Never hardcode secrets, API keys, or credentials — use environment variables / existing config patterns.
- Sanitize and validate all user input on any new endpoint or form handler.
- Follow existing auth/permission checks already used elsewhere in the app — don't skip them for "simplicity."

## Testing
- If the project has an existing test suite, add or update tests only for the code you changed — don't write tests for unrelated code.
- Do not remove or weaken existing tests to make something pass.

## Communication
- Before making changes across multiple files, briefly list which files you plan to touch and why, and wait for approval.
- If a request is ambiguous, ask a clarifying question instead of guessing and implementing the wrong thing.
- Flag (but don't silently fix) any bugs you notice outside the scope of the current task.

## Git hygiene
- Do not run git commit or git push unless explicitly asked to.
- Do not modify .env, CI/CD configs, or deployment files unless that is the specific task.
